.Dd October 7, 2026
.Dt ZONEFILE-GO 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm zonefile-go
.Nd generate Unbound and NSD zone data
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl n
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl o Ar path
.Op Fl s Ar serialfile
.Op Fl t Cm unbound | nsd | dhcpd
.Nm
.Fl V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads forward zones, hosts and reverse networks from a configuration file
in the format described in
.Xr zonefile.conf 5
and writes the zone data for
.Xr unbound 8
or
.Xr nsd 8 ,
or the configuration of
.Xr dhcpd 8 .
PTR records are generated from the addresses of the hosts, for the
networks declared as reverse zones.
.Pp
The configuration is checked completely before anything is written.
All errors are reported, one per line, in the form
.Ar file : Ns Ar line : message .
Warnings have the same form with
.Dq warning:
before the message; they do not stop the output from being written.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f Ar file
Read the configuration from
.Ar file .
If
.Ar file
is
.Sq - ,
read it from standard input; relative include paths are then resolved
against the current directory.
The default is
.Pa /etc/zonefile.conf .
.It Fl n
Configtest mode.
Only check the configuration file for validity and print
.Dq configuration OK
to standard error.
Nothing is written and the serial is not changed.
.It Fl o Ar path
Where to write the output.
For
.Cm unbound
and
.Cm dhcpd ,
.Ar path
is a file, written atomically; without
.Fl o
the output goes to standard output.
For
.Cm nsd ,
.Ar path
is a directory, by default
.Pa nsd .
.It Fl s Ar serialfile
Keep the serial and content hash of each zone in
.Ar serialfile ,
see
.Sx Serial numbers .
The default is
.Pa /var/db/zonefile-go.serial .
.It Fl t Cm unbound | nsd | dhcpd
The output format.
The default is
.Cm unbound .
.Bl -tag -width unbound
.It Cm unbound
A fragment of
.Xr unbound.conf 5
with a
.Ic server
clause and one
.Ic local-zone
with its
.Ic local-data
and
.Ic local-data-ptr
statements per zone.
.It Cm dhcpd
A complete
.Xr dhcpd.conf 5
with one
.Ic subnet
declaration per
.Ic dhcp
block and a
.Ic host
declaration with a fixed address for every MAC address of a host.
The serial file is neither read nor written.
.It Cm nsd
The file
.Pa zones.conf ,
with one
.Ic zone
clause per zone for inclusion into
.Xr nsd.conf 5 ,
and one zone file per zone in the subdirectory
.Pa master .
Every file is written atomically, the zone files before
.Pa zones.conf .
Zone files named in the previous
.Pa zones.conf
whose zones are no longer configured are removed afterwards and reported
on standard error.
Other files in
.Pa master
are left alone.
.El
.It Fl V
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Ss Serial numbers
A zone gets a new serial only when its content changes.
The serial file holds one line per zone with its name, the serial it was
last written with, and a SHA-256 hash of its NSD zone file with the serial
set to 0.
The hash is taken from the NSD form whichever format is written, so
.Cm unbound
and
.Cm nsd
output share the serial file and give a zone the same serial.
.Pp
If the hash of a zone is unchanged, the zone keeps its serial.
If it changed, or the zone is new, the zone gets a serial in the format
.Ar YYYYMMDDnn :
the current date in UTC with
.Ar nn
set to 00, or its previous serial plus one if that is larger.
Several changes on one day therefore count up, and a serial never goes
back.
A zone with a fixed
.Ic serial
keeps it.
Zones that are no longer configured drop out of the serial file.
.Pp
The output is always written in full, also when no serial changed.
The serial file is updated only after the output has been written.
It is the only record of the serials: if it is lost, the serials start
again from the current date, which may be lower than the serials already
served.
.Pp
A serial file of an earlier version, which holds a single number, is
taken over: that number is the previous serial of every zone, so each
zone gets one new serial on the first run.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/usr/local/etc/zonefile.conf" -compact
.It Pa /etc/zonefile.conf
Default configuration file.
.It Pa /var/db/zonefile-go.serial
Default serial file.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check a configuration:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zonefile-go -n -f /etc/zonefile.conf
.Ed
.Pp
Generate local data for
.Xr unbound 8
and reload it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zonefile-go -o /var/unbound/etc/zones.conf
# unbound-control reload
.Ed
.Pp
with
.Bd -literal -offset indent
include: "/var/unbound/etc/zones.conf"
.Ed
.Pp
in
.Pa /var/unbound/etc/unbound.conf .
.Pp
Generate zones for
.Xr nsd 8
and reload them:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zonefile-go -t nsd -o /var/nsd/zones
# nsd-control reconfig && nsd-control reload
.Ed
.Pp
with
.Bd -literal -offset indent
include: "/var/nsd/zones/zones.conf"
.Ed
.Pp
in
.Pa /var/nsd/etc/nsd.conf .
The
.Ic zonefile
paths in
.Pa zones.conf
start with
.Pa master/ ,
so
.Ic zonesdir
in
.Xr nsd.conf 5
has to be the output directory,
.Pa /var/nsd/zones
here.
.Pp
Write the configuration of
.Xr dhcpd 8 ,
check it and restart the daemon:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zonefile-go -t dhcpd -o /etc/dhcpd.conf
# dhcpd -n -c /etc/dhcpd.conf && rcctl restart dhcpd
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dhcpd.conf 5 ,
.Xr nsd.conf 5 ,
.Xr unbound.conf 5 ,
.Xr zonefile.conf 5 ,
.Xr dhcpd 8 ,
.Xr nsd 8 ,
.Xr unbound 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
succeeds zonefile, written in Python, and zonefile-rs, written in Rust,
which read YAML or TOML.
For the same zones it writes the same records, except that it rejects
duplicate records instead of writing them twice.
